  "query_preview": "/Through Tuesday/...\nIssued at 116 PM MDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nThe center of a weakening upper level ridge sits near the CO/NM\nborder this afternoon. Water vapor satellite shows modest amounts\nof moisture rotating around the western/northern edge of the\nridge. That's led to the development of a few showers and a couple\nof thunderstorms - mainly across Park County so far. Despite the\nincrease in mid-level moisture, we don't expect much more than\nsome gusty showers across the plains where deep mixing and\ninverted-V profiles are still noted on ACARS soundings today.\nExpect a fairly similar story to yesterday for the Denver metro,\nwith a couple of showers and gusty outflow winds, with little in\nthe way of meaningful rainfall.\n\nThe ridge is expected to continue weakening as it pushes off to\nthe southeast on Wednesday and Thursday, with broad south-\nsouthwesterly flow allowing monsoonal moisture to finally make\nit's long-awaited return. This moisture may be a tad late to\narrive for most of the lower elevations tomorrow, but we should \nsee good coverage of storms across the higher elevations, with\nchances diminishing quickly from west to east across the urban\ncorridor. Some guidance hints at a weak MCV-like feature\ndeveloping tomorrow evening that would at least encourage a few\nmore storms across the lower elevations... but will be worth\nwatching to see if that feature persists among the 00z HREF\nmembers. There is also a somewhat conditional slight risk of\nsevere weather per SPC across the northeastern plains for the\nafternoon and evening hours if convection manages to develop.\n\nThursday appears to have most of the ingredients in place for\nscattered to widespread rainfall across most of the foothills and\nadjacent plains. A weak surface cold front is expected to push\nthrough late Wednesday night or Thursday morning, with cooling\nmid-level temperatures as well. Forecast highs are only in the\nupper 70s to low 80s, with dew points increasing to 55-60F. Most\nmodel soundings show sufficient instability, minimal capping, and\ncontinued upslope through the day, along with some sort of weak\nmid-level shortwave tracking through the area during the\nafternoon. As previous AFDs have noted, forecast soundings largely\nsupport heavy rainfall across the area with precipitable water \nincreasing to ~1.25\", modestly deep warm cloud layers, and \nrelatively weak cloud layer mean wind. This is not all that unlike\nsome of the environments we saw in late July, but the subtle \nsynoptic forcing at least leads to a little more optimism with \nthis setup.\n\nThe overall pattern does not change significantly for Friday.\nContinued easterly surface flow will allow for dew points to\nremain fairly constant, with slow moisture advection into the\nregion. Another weak shortwave will encourage another round of\nwidespread showers and storms across the higher elevations,\nspreading east into the I-25 corridor and plains Friday\nafternoon/evening. \n\nPrecipitation chances will stick around through the weekend with\nabove normal moisture remaining over the area. The broad ridge\nacross the southern CONUS is expected to slowly strengthen and\nshift back towards the west, which will eventually start to cut\noff our moisture feed. In the meantime another weak front or two\ncould promote additional chances for showers/storms on Saturday,\nwith higher PoPs sinking south of our CWA by Sunday.\n\nThe ridge will make an attempt to build back across our forecast\narea early next week, leading to zonal flow redeveloping across\nmost of the state. That should limit storm chances for a couple of\ndays (Monday/Tuesday) with temperatures also warming back up\ncloser to 90F. Looking beyond the forecast period... most ECMWF\nensemble members push the ridge back into a more favorable\nposition for monsoon moisture to work back into the region, with\nbroad support from the GEFS and CMCE.",
